WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.630
 Delicious Revolution is brought to you by us, by me and Chelsea, specifically by our consulting business.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:11.630
 We provide participatory research, communications, and creative work to organizations that are building food movements.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.450
 What's your food movement? Let us know and get in touch.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:23.930
 DeliciousRevolutionShow.com

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:30.410
 With food, we talk about issues. It's like what MFK Fisher said about feeding our many hungers.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:35.370
 How we treat our food system, how we consume food is a reflection of how we are in the world.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:41.690
 And I think by helping people to remember those connections, we bring even issues that are far away.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.830
 You know, even issues that seem far away, like up close.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:50.260
 Somehow or another, you know, the conversation has moved towards some people have taste, quote unquote,

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.660
 and some people don't. And we look to Yelp reviews, or we look to Michelin stars,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:02.940
 and we start to give away some of our power around that. And you know, my very strong desire is to remind

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.140
 people that this system is created by us, that the agricultural system that is in place,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.640
 that the food distribution system that is in place is something that we actively participate in,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.760
 and that we can reshape with every meal. And, you know, to say, eat differently, to look at these

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:24.640
 choices and realize like that you are, you are reshaping this or shaping the system that we have,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.840
 and that your taste matters. The democratization of this is key to me, because I really do feel like

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.960
 more and more we see this kind of separation between those who kind of know where it's at and

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.640
 understand food and have a sophisticated view of it, and then the rest of us. And I'm unwilling to accept

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:53.260
 that as as a reasonable response. Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food,

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:59.240
 culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills. And I'm Devin Sampson. We talk with people who work in and

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:05.170
 think about all aspects of food, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists bringing you in-depth

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:09.730
 conversations with some amazing people. On this third season of Delicious Revolution,

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:15.730
 we're bringing you stories and perspectives from unseen places in food systems, going behind kitchen

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:22.050
 doors into underground nests of native bees under the waves and to the far away origins of flavors we

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:28.750
 love, just to name a few. I'm speaking with people who work with food in places we normally cannot see

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:34.510
 or don't notice. It's a season of unseen stories of food. You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:39.800
 iTunes or your favorite podcast app. Just search for Delicious Revolution wherever you get your podcasts,

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.600
 and you'll find us. Our website is deliciousrevolutionshow.com, where we have pictures,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.380
 links, and more information about all the people on our show.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:56.160
 Simran Sethi is a journalist and educator focused on food, sustainability, and social change.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.680
 She was named the Environmental Messenger by Vanity Fair, a top 10 eco-hero of the planet by the UK's

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.280
 Independent, designated one of the top eight women saving the planet by Mary Claire.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.710
 Simran is the author of Bread, Wine, Chocolate: The Slow Loss of Foods We Love. It's a beautiful book,

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:17.150
 personal, and it's the story of changes in food and agriculture told through bread, wine, chocolate,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:22.190
 and coffee and beer. She is an associate of the University of Melbourne's Sustainable Society

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:26.910
 Institute in Australia and contributor to Orion Magazine and a recent visiting scholar at the

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:33.440
 Cocoa Research Center in St. Augustine, Trinidad. Here's Chelsea's interview with Simran Sethi.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:42.950
 Hey Simran, welcome to the show. Thank you. It's great to have you here today. I guess I just wanted

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:50.050
 to start by, I was preparing, looking at all the things you've done, and you've really spanned a

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:56.590
 lot of issues about environmental subjects. And what brought you to food? You know, I think food is

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:05.350
 kind of the connective tissue of my life. And food was always there. I just didn't realize how I could weave

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:11.720
 it into everything I cared about around social justice and around environmental concerns. Early

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:17.320
 on, it was sort of separate from that. But what I, what increasingly happened over time was that,

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.280
 you know, I was, I was an academic and I was teaching classes on environmental journalism

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.440
 and started to move them, you know, to filter kind of the whole, the whole story of the environment

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.720
 through food and agriculture. And then I found my reporting and journalism was really tending toward

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.400
 food and agriculture as well. And I realized that that's how I wanted to engage with the world and

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:45.440
 do my work that, you know, in the career I've had in environmental, you know, journalism and advocacy,

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:51.290
 I've talked about myriad environmental issues, but I don't think people have a deep connection to

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:56.570
 something like the electrical grid. Even though, of course we cherish light, we need electricity. We

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:03.390
 couldn't have this conversation without it. But the intimacy of food and the ability for food to

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:10.990
 transcend so many divides, be it political, racial, you know, gender, uh, geographical, I feel like is,

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:19.040
 is something sacred and is something humbling and is where I want to spend the rest of my life doing my work.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.710
 Yeah. Yeah. And in your book, I feel like you keep coming back to that, right? You talk about,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:31.230
 you talk about love and you talk about connection and you talk about relationships and it feels, um,

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:37.370
 for me, like what I kept coming back to in your book is the sense of intimacy with what that means.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.930
 Do you feel like that's a thread that kind of follows through with your...

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.130
 Absolutely. I, you know, I, I set off to write a book about the loss of agricultural biodiversity,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:51.090
 which is effectively the loss of diversity and everything that makes food possible from

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:58.290
 the soil to the seeds to pollinators onto crops and livestock and, and aquatic life. But what I

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:04.810
 realized through this journey, which it took me, um, gosh, it took me five years to get across six

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:10.730
 continents to do the research, um, was that it was really a book about love, that the connection to

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:17.950
 food was really one about relationship and that if I could bring it back for myself and for others to

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:24.030
 that central place that, um, you know, not only do I firmly believe this kind of anecdotally, but,

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:29.230
 but research bears this out that, that we will be able to affect change, you know? And that was kind

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.790
 of something that I was really bumping up against. And, and some of the other environmental discourse

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.550
 was it's like, gosh, how many more times do I have to tell you to weatherstrip your house?

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:41.790
 You know, like what, what there's, I was on the Oprah Winfrey show and I remember the third time I

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.270
 was on the show, they kind of bring me out for earth day and the producer called me up a couple

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:51.390
 of days ahead of time. And she said, Simran, is there anything new or sexy and weatherstripping?

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:58.430
 I was like, nope, still just weatherstripping, you know, nothing there got, you know, just do it.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:03.820
 You know, it's really great to do and saves you money and helps the environment, you know?

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:09.820
 And, and I realized like, that's it, you know, with food to talk about issues. It's like what

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:15.820
 NFK Fisher said about feeding our many hungers. You know, this is how we treat our food system,

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:21.020
 how we consume food is a reflection of how we are in the world. And I think by helping people to

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:26.740
 remember those connections, we bring even issues that are far away, you know, even issues that seem

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:28.260
 far away, like up close.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:35.550
 Yeah. And I mean, I think it's a visceral process, right? So there's no, there's no way

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:40.590
 of avoiding it. And everybody's an expert because you have to do it all the time, right? Whether,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:46.170
 whether you love it or whether you don't love it. And, um, something that felt really interesting

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:53.500
 to me was thinking about this. Well, uh, five years doesn't seem like that long of a time to me

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:59.230
 to like explore all of these, all of these could be a whole book, right? All of these subjects,

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:05.830
 they're so complicated. There's so many things to, um, these commodity chains and the relationships

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:12.760
 and how people produce, uh, you know, bread and wine and chocolate and beer. And I mean, I got,

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:17.160
 I got towards the end and I was like, why can't the octopus chapter be as long as the rest of them?

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:23.720
 Come on. Because my editor wouldn't let me, I actually gave a manuscript to that's about the,

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:30.440
 the book runs about 300 pages. I submitted a 400 page book and they, the very first response I got

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.230
 was, I mean, and I'm using the page count instead of the word count, but it was like, you do realize

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:39.910
 it was supposed to be two 50, right? Like that I kind of already overdid, but I want to go back to

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:45.030
 what you said about everyone being an expert. I think that's actually one of the challenges about food

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:51.940
 is that, you know, taste is something that we all do and we all have, but, but somehow or another,

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:57.470
 um, you know, the, the conversation has moved towards some people have taste quote unquote,

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:02.830
 and some people don't, you know, and, and we look to Yelp reviews or we look to Michelin stars and we

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:10.970
 start to give away some of our power around that. And, you know, my very strong, uh, desire is to

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:16.890
 remind people that this system is created by us, that the agricultural system that is in place,

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:22.310
 that the food distribution system that is in place is something that we actively participate in and

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:27.970
 that we can reshape with every meal. And I mean this so sincerely. And I feel like in so many realms,

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:32.850
 it's like, well, you know, I don't know what kind of impact I can have on the electrical grid,

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:37.490
 you know, to sort of use that example again, you know, or I don't feel any sense of power when,

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.570
 you know, the response is like, right, you're elected official. You know, as a matter of fact,

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:46.210
 I feel disempowered by that kind of action often like in this, in this day and age, but,

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:51.710
 but, you know, to say eat differently, to look at these choices and realize like that you are,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:57.070
 you are reshaping this or shaping the system that we have and, and that your taste matters.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:03.130
 The democratization of this is key to me because I really do feel like more and more we see this

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:08.890
 kind of separation between those who kind of know where it's at and understand food and have a

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:13.930
 sophisticated view of it. And then the rest of us, you know, and, and I'm unwilling to accept that as,

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:21.370
 as a reasonable response. Yeah. I mean, there's, there's so much to unpack in that, right? Of course,

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:27.360
 about who gets to make these kinds of decisions and, and also who's right. You know,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:35.340
 I think that I felt like I really wanted to know, like, how did you decide to start tasting things?

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:43.070
 I realized, I love this question. So just to, to give a little bit of background in the book,

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:48.990
 there are tasting guides and flavor wheels, and I unpack how every one of our senses plays into the

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:53.230
 experience of flavor that culminates in our mouth, right? What is, what we commonly say,

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:58.750
 like, what does something taste like is really like really the kind of fine point of smell and taste

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:03.970
 coming together. And then all those other senses factoring in. And I realized it went back again to

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:08.770
 so much of what I had read, where there would be these kind of edicts, you know, like, Hey,

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:13.330
 if you want to participate in the food system, you got to grow your own food and you got to

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:17.570
 ferment something and you got to look up a bunch of stuff in the dictionary to figure out what I'm

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:23.350
 talking about, you know, and you have to eat differently. And, and that's all reasonable,

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:29.530
 I suppose. But for me, you know, I, I wanted to write, I didn't have in my mind as the, like,

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:35.880
 as my reader, um, someone who was reading the New Yorker, you know, and who's like dining at the,

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:40.040
 the French laundry, you know, I had someone who's just like trying to feed her kids and,

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:44.520
 you know, buys food in the regular grocery store. You know, these are the people I lived beside in

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:49.000
 Lawrence, Kansas, when I taught at the university. And these are people I love. And I know these people

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:54.120
 have great intentions and may never have been given the opportunity or the tools to make

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.080
 different kinds of decisions. And, um, and in realizing that, and even some of my earlier work

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:03.720
 around asking people to change their behaviors, it's really hard. And it's even more difficult when

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:09.430
 you don't give people a guide, you know, to doing it. And, and I realized as I was exploring these

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:14.650
 foods, I tell the loss, I talk about the loss of agricultural biodiversity through bread, wine,

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:20.190
 coffee, chocolate, and beer. And, um, and, you know, when I first started tasting coffee,

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:24.270
 for example, it's like, well, I, I thought I was a coffee sophisticate, just knowing that I preferred

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:29.110
 a latte to a cappuccino, you know, like I just, I thought I knew where it was at, you know? And then

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:33.770
 here I am sitting with coffee roasters and they're saying like, do you, do you taste the jasmine? You

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:37.690
 know, do you smell the jasmine? I should say, do you taste these qualities? And I was like,

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:43.350
 I taste coffee guys. Like I don't taste any of that. And I realized what a long journey it was and how

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:49.110
 in getting more intimate with my food, in taking time to experience it and to learning the steps

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:55.690
 on how to taste these things, I would actually be able to understand that the taste of place existed

00:12:55.690 --> 00:13:03.110
 in everything. I would actually be able to discern why the taste of a craft chocolate or a craft beer

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:08.510
 was different than a Snickers bar or a Bud Light. You know, I mean, on the surface, of course you can

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:14.870
 experience these things, but I wanted to do a deep dive and gosh, it has been the most joyous part of

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:22.110
 the journey is discovering deliciousness. And, you know, I, I start off the book with a poem by Derek

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:27.450
 Wolcott, uh, love after love. And he, he ends the poem with, you know, with the edict to feast on your

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:32.190
 life. And that's, you know, that's the mandate of the book. That's the mandate of my work. It's,

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:39.390
 it's get your hands in there and dig in and lick your fingers and feast, you know, whether it's,

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:45.830
 uh, I don't know whether it's your Kentucky fried chicken or, you know, your, your grass fed, you

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:51.830
 know, free range, whatever, like know where it comes from, be informed and own those choices as

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.350
 you put whatever it is you're putting in your mouth there.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:14:01.200
 So what does that mean in every day? Like there, of course, everybody has these experiences of diving

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:07.760
 deep sometimes, right? Like, um, I think about first birthday cakes as a perfect example because

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:13.000
 of the smashing the cake into your face. It's like this, I guess I don't, I wouldn't, I wouldn't put

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:17.280
 that in the same category of two year old. No, because I don't, I don't, I don't know what that

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:25.050
 little brain is processing for me. That awareness is about, uh, not only a sensory experience, but an

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:32.090
 experience of the longer story, you know, not the birthday cake in the moment, but the story of where

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:36.920
 that week came from. And I don't, I don't do this with every food, I mean, you know, but, but to choose

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:43.860
 a few and to recognize they like what they represent is emblematic of a bigger system, you know, um, to

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:49.870
 me is what gives me power. And, and so like, what do you, what does that mean for your friends in

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:56.790
 Lawrence, Kansas or your friends who are coffee farmers? Um, well, I think what it means for my

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:04.430
 friends in Lawrence or in San Francisco or, you know, in Jakarta or wherever is just that, um, to pay

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:10.970
 attention to something is to love it more, you know, to recognize its evanescence, to recognize

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:18.740
 it's, um, it's, it's, it's kind of longer trajectory to, to realize how much it took to get it to

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:25.950
 your cup or your plate. You know, I, I, I am truly in awe of, uh, I counted with Peter Giuliano,

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:30.590
 who's the director of the specialty coffee association of America. I was like, do a back of an envelope

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:35.670
 kind of like running, run the numbers for me, Peter, how many hands are involved in getting a cup of

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:41.450
 coffee to my, you know, to my kitchen. And I was, I was really thinking about the metaphor of hands

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:46.910
 cause I hand grind my own coffee. And every morning I'm like, Oh, I hate this. Like I could just use an

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:52.530
 electric grinder and I'd be done by now, you know? And it was really that symbolism of saying, and he

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:56.470
 counted it off for me and he's like, you know, there's more than this, but, but the modest estimate

00:15:56.470 --> 00:16:02.010
 would be 18 sets of hands. And to go back to the place where coffee began, which is, um,

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:07.770
 the center of origin for coffee is Ethiopia and South Sudan. So I was in Ethiopia for three weeks,

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:13.970
 um, spending time with those farmers and seeing firsthand what their challenges are has humbled

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:18.910
 me. And it's made me grateful for a cup of coffee in a way that I couldn't have experienced before.

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:25.990
 Now everyone can't hop on a plane for every food, but what they can do is, uh, well, read the book or

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:31.810
 read something else or watch, you know, Anthony Bourdain or whatever takes you back to source. And

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:37.250
 then kind of realize that happens with everything. And for me as someone who I don't like the term

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:43.370
 foodie, but it's very food ish, you know, and it's kind of navigated my life through food. Um,

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:49.970
 I was stunned by how little I knew. And I was stunned by how much more joy I have in the experience of

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:57.510
 every food. Um, because I now am more informed about how it kind of came into being and how it got to me.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:02.230
 And I mean that for everything. I mean that for the mint Milano cookie. I mean that for

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:08.470
 the carrot, the heirloom carrot, I mean it for every single thing that passes, you know, onto my

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:15.400
 table that there's a story there. And even if it's a fleeting consideration, um, for five seconds in

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:21.350
 the morning or flipping over a package or thanking the farmer at the farmer's market, that it, it binds

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:27.810
 me to them and it helps me to realize that, um, that the whole world shows up on my plate.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:33.810
 Can you talk a little bit more about this difference between feeling awe or curiosity

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:36.750
 for the world versus like a set of you shoulds?

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:47.710
 I think the, the kind of balance between the shoulds and the curiosity for me is this, um,

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:53.540
 curiosity is leading with an open mind and an open heart and should means that you've already

00:17:53.540 --> 00:18:00.470
 predetermined what the answer and the destination is. You should do this. Um, you must, in order to be

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:07.790
 sustainable, you must do that. And I, I write in the book that I try very hard to live by my values,

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:13.430
 but I also realized that, um, I am a victim of circumstance. You know, when I'm stuck in an

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:18.430
 airport and I'm hungry, I'll eat something I ordinarily wouldn't eat when I'm on, you know,

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:23.490
 an airplane and the flight attendant will not refill my reusable bottle. I will take the plastic cup

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:29.130
 and, you know, but that isn't to say I didn't put that reusable bottle in my bag and carry it with

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:33.950
 me wherever I was going. I think, um, when it comes to food, it was a real epiphany for me

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:45.000
 that, um, few weeks before the book came out, my father passed away. And so here I was expected to,

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:52.290
 first of all, birth a book while I'm grieving the death of my father, which is freaky enough. Um,

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:58.120
 but also that, you know, the mandate of the book, like I just said, is like feast on your life. It's

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:05.250
 not, it's not like grow herbs or ferment or, you know, whatever it's like, enjoy and really savor,

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:12.200
 participate in what you have. And the irony in being so grief stricken that I lost my appetite

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:16.900
 and then kind of having to go around and do interviews and talk to people and say like,

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:23.590
 get in there and savor, you know, we can save, we can transform our food system through savoring.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:31.090
 And here I was like not eating. And, um, you know, the book came out on November 10th.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:37.660
 So Thanksgiving was shortly thereafter. And my aunt was making Thanksgiving dinner and she,

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:42.130
