00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:56.730
 And that my anchor, like the place where I was finding joy in that moment was in the dehydrated

00:19:56.730 --> 00:20:04.410
 onion, like cream of mushroom soup, you know, like whateverness, you know, that's what brought me joy

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:11.330
 that it was not like, you must do things this way. What I am suggesting is like in being curious and

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:18.240
 in being grateful, um, we approach our plate, we approach our relationships in a different way.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:26.770
 And through that, we view them differently. And we, I think, respond differently. If, um, if food is

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:33.490
 simply caloric nourishment, then we can all just drink Soylent, you know, and call it a day. Um, but I

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:41.400
 think we all know food is memory, food is story, food is identity. And, um, and using food as a lens to

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:49.690
 understand how the story of who we are is changed. I think, um, for me, at least reshapes how I look at

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:57.730
 it, you know, so it's, it's nourishment in the broader sense. It's, um, it's, you know, what the

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:04.670
 monodiet, you know, the monocultures we sow in fields means in the larger sense that depletion in a

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:12.080
 field, in the soil, the loss of biodiversity, you know, right there at the beginning is about, uh, a

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:18.340
 loss in us, a depletion in us. Um, and that's what I want to reclaim, you know, and that's what I think,

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:22.480
 um, a broad prescriptive, like feast on your life allows for.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:29.990
 Yeah. It, that reminds me of, um, Devin and I lived in Mexico and worked in a small town there for a

