00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:14.470
 me from Oakland to Berkeley, you know, and I'm telling him, Oh, I'm going to Ethiopia in a couple

00:30:14.470 --> 00:30:19.470
 of weeks. And he said, my family's in Addis. Do you want to stay with them? And like the kind of

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:25.300
 audacity of the offer. And then the audacity of me saying, I would love to, you know, and, um,

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:30.940
 the generosity it took in him, the courage it took in me. And then that place where we meet.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:39.650
 And, um, I, I wasn't interested in stepping into these scenarios and then stepping out again

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:47.730
 and giving some sort of, uh, like outsider perspective. Um, I wanted intimacy because I don't

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:53.760
 I think, I think the reason some of those conversations, books, whatever treatises on

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:57.420
 food, the reason they haven't worked is because they haven't touched the heart. You know, they've

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:02.740
 been these deeply intellectual exercises. And I remember even reading every book. Honestly,

00:31:02.740 --> 00:31:12.320
 I'd read on, on chocolate. Um, none of them gave me like the steamy, sexy heat of the cacao forest.

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:21.820
 Like, oh my God, like that place assaults you with just juiciness and just, oh, you know, I was like,

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:27.270
 why didn't I know this? I've read every book I could get my hands on. Why, why didn't any of them

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:32.630
 get in there and tell me about the guts of this and the juice of this? And that's what I wanted to

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:38.510
 do. And sometimes my questions to people seemed on the surface audacious, you know, some of them,

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:44.110
 or my, my requests of people, you know, Hey, coffee farmer, show me your hands, you know? And some of

