00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:30.280
 but I am now willing to hold that complexity and to recognize the humanity and people that before,

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:37.330
 um, I didn't see. It's not that they weren't there, but that I, I, in many ways, like dismissed

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:43.980
 because they weren't part of my conversation. And in doing research on kind of, um, why it looks like

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:49.920
 people aren't acting. I was really struck by, um, research from two behavioral economists at Duke

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:56.330
 university who talk about the finite pool of worry. And they say, you know, in order to get people to

00:39:56.330 --> 00:40:01.070
 really like kind of engage with what you care about, you either have to speak within the frame of their

00:40:01.070 --> 00:40:07.570
 existing cares, or you have to displace one of their worries. And, um, that was so epiphanic for me

00:40:07.570 --> 00:40:13.750
 because I then was able to turn that on myself and realize, yes, I care about Syrian refugees.

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:19.050
 Yes. I care about polar bears on ice flows. But if you think that's top of mind, when I wake up in

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:24.390
 the morning, it's not, and it's not because I'm dumb. It's not because I'm evil. It's not because

00:40:24.390 --> 00:40:29.550
 I don't care. It's because my little brain can only hold so much. And today I need to worry about

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:34.470
 doing this interview with you, getting myself breakfast, talking to my sister while she's in Hawaii,

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:38.350
 you know, meeting my book deadline, my deadline for the thing I'm writing today,

00:40:38.450 --> 00:40:43.830
 going to the grocery store, like, you know that. And I just like, I'm sorry. I didn't do anything

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:49.310
 for the Syrian refugees today, you know? And, and, and I mean that sincerely, but what I also mean is

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:55.110
 that like, I learned that I wanted to talk about issues in a different way in ways that would become

00:40:55.110 --> 00:41:01.330
 intimate. So I will talk about war through food. You know, I will talk about reconciliation through food.

00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:05.910
 I will talk about my heartbreaking. And I just, uh, worked on a piece that, you know,

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:09.770
 where I asked the question, like, what's the flavor of resilience? You know, what's the flavor

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:15.630
 of beginning again? I have a broken heart broken from my father, broken from a relationship that was,

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:20.350
 you know, an intimate relationship. And like, how do I move on? You know, when I think these are things

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:26.620
 that like transcend the divide and they, they glue us back together, like I said before. Um,

00:41:26.620 --> 00:41:33.880
 but that's, that's, I don't know. That's to me what I'm working on. How do we heal? What's torn? How do

00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:40.320
 we build the bridges? And, um, I believe we do it through food. Well, thank you so much. That's a

00:41:40.320 --> 00:41:47.110
 really nice place to end. I'm so glad I had a chance to talk to you today. Me too. I'm glad I had a chance

00:41:47.110 --> 00:41:52.240
 to explore your questions too. Cause that's, you know, that's half of it right there, putting it out

00:41:52.240 --> 00:42:02.920
 there and hoping someone really hears you, you know, really gets you. And, um, you did. So thanks.

